			<item>
				<title>Dynamic array and linked list</title>
				<link>https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/datastructures/sections/list/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/datastructures/sections/list/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;dynamic-array-and-linked-list&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Dynamic array and linked list&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#dynamic-array-and-linked-list&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;array&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Dynamic array&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#array&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A dynamic array is typically used to implement the abstract data type &lt;a href=&#34;https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/abstractdatatypes/sections/list/&#34;&gt;list&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;structure&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Structure&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#structure&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote class=&#34;book-hint warning&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;dynamic array&lt;/strong&gt; stores the elements of a list in a regular array $A$, and keeps track of the size of the list.&#xA;If the size of the list exceeds the capacity of $A$, then the content of $A$ is copied into a larger array $A&amp;rsquo;$,&#xA;and $A$ is replaced with $A&amp;rsquo;$.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Hash table</title>
				<link>https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/datastructures/sections/hash/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/datastructures/sections/hash/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;hash-table&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Hash table&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#hash-table&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A hash table is a data structure often used to implement an &lt;a href=&#34;https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/abstractdatatypes/sections/map/&#34;&gt;associative array&lt;/a&gt; (e.g. a Java &lt;code&gt;Map&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;!--We will start with this usage, before discussing how is can be used for other scenarios.--&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;associative-array&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Associative array&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#associative-array&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote class=&#34;book-hint info&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Reminder.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; An &lt;a href=&#34;https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/abstractdatatypes/sections/map/&#34;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;associative array&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; is a finite set of key-value pairs, where keys are distinct.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote class=&#34;book-hint warning&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Terminology.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&#xA;A key-value pair in an associative array is also called an &lt;strong&gt;entry&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
			<item>
				<title>Binary search tree</title>
				<link>https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/datastructures/sections/tree/</link>
				<pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<guid>https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/datastructures/sections/tree/</guid>
				<description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;binary-search-tree&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Binary search tree&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#binary-search-tree&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;Binary Search Tree (BST)&lt;/strong&gt; is another data structure used to store an &lt;a href=&#34;https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/abstractdatatypes/sections/map/&#34;&gt;associative array&lt;/a&gt; (e.g. a Java &lt;code&gt;Map&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote class=&#34;book-hint warning&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Definition.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&#xA;A &lt;strong&gt;BST&lt;/strong&gt; is a &lt;a href=&#34;https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/recursion/sections/nary/#binaryTrees&#34;&gt;binary tree&lt;/a&gt; such that each node&amp;rsquo;s label is:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;larger than the label of its left child (if any), and&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;smaller than the label of its right child (if any).&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Equivalently, a BST is a binary tree whose nodes are sorted w.r.t. &lt;a href=&#34;https://unibz-oofp-25-26.github.io/docs/recursion/sections/nary/#traversal&#34;&gt;in-order traversal&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
			</item>
